The experiential advantage (Van Boven &amp; Gilovich, 2003) suggests that people who&#13;
spend their money on experiences, rather than material purchases, are happier. In addition&#13;
to happiness, people also receive more identity (Carter &amp; Gilovich, 2012) and relatedness&#13;
(Kumar &amp; Gilovich, 2015) satisfaction from their experiential purchases. The goal of the&#13;
current project is to replicate the two latter benefits, while extending the replication&#13;
efforts to examine potential boundary conditions across different types of consumers and&#13;
experiential products. Specifically, recent research suggests that the level of advantage&#13;
may be dependent on an individual's preferential spending habits: stronger for&#13;
experientialists and weaker or non-existent for materialists (Zhang et al., 2014). Finally,&#13;
the present research addresses the arbitrary dichotomy of purchase type, including&#13;
purchases that possess characteristics of both experiential and material purchases&#13;
(Guevarra &amp; Howell, 2015). The goal of this research project is to provide a more&#13;
nuanced recommendation to each unique consumer.

