summary: There are multiple ways to report risk scale results. Varela et al. (2014) found that risk from Static-99R was interpreted differently by prospective jurors based on risk level (high vs low) and an interaction between risk level and risk communication format (categorical, probability estimate, and risk ratio). They did not address participant personality characteristics. This study (N = 166 community members) directly replicated Varela et al. and added the individual variables ‘cognitive structure’ (i.e., need for cognition) and ‘attitudinal affect’ (i.e., attitudes toward sex offenders, authoritarianism). We replicated Varela et al’s main effect for risk level, but not for communication format. We did not replicate the interaction between risk level and communication format. We found no zero-order correlation between need for cognition and risk perception and excluded it from the further analyses. In a regression analysis, adding the two attitudinal affect variables increased explained variance to from 9% to 31%, suggesting risk perception may be more about the person receiving the information than the information itself. We also found a significant interaction between attitudes towards sex offenders and risk level. Our results suggest risk perception might be better understood if personality factors are additionally considered, particularly attitudes towards sex offenders.

